The strongest surviving target in this opening is a verified isotope intervention inside an existing covalent structure: change the local mass arrangement, restore the elemental geometry, and independently establish what carbon was transferred. I developed a common force-and-scattering model for that operation. It produces a physically specified mass-readout signal without assigning isotope contrast or reaction probabilities.The same calculation also weakens the small motif’s materials case. Its most visible predicted vibrational change is largely reproducible by scanning around a stationary isotope. The smaller contribution that depends on the relative arrangement of two labels is substantially harder to measure. I am retaining the mass-transfer witness as the useful opening and withdrawing the small pristine-graphene motif’s optical response as a justification for a material device.The returning-mediator reachability result, observation limits, processing costs and failed cross-energy calibration remain conditional results at their previous scope. Nothing below supplies the missing carbon-transfer chemistry.A concrete operation with a paid return and parking pathThe proposed intervention uses the retained 24-exchange closed Si route, which returns Si to its starting site while cycling three carbon positions, a,b,fa,b,fa,b,f. For the physical-response calculation, the diagnostic input has 13C^{13}\mathrm C13C at aaa and a fixed spectator position ccc; the other carbons are 12C^{12}\mathrm C12C. The intended output has the heavy labels at f,cf,cf,c.That input is a specified test configuration, not an acquired preparation or an assumed success probability.I added an explicit 16-hop Si parking path, ending approximately 2 nm from the intervention region. Its final token permutation has disjoint support from the carbon cycle. This permits the following paired comparison:Park Si and acquire the first read.Return Si, perform the closed carbon cycle, and park Si along the same path.Acquire the second read.Under faithful exchanges, both read states have the same elemental structure and the same rearrangement along the parking path. Their intended difference is the isotope arrangement in the intervention region.The exchange counts are consequential:ComparisonIdeal successful Si–C exchangesBefore/edit/after, ending with Si parked72Same comparison, followed by returning Si to its initial site88Before/edit/undo/reference, ending with Si parked128These counts exclude unsuccessful attempts, recovery, initial isotope identification, registration and imaging. I have not converted them into operating times using the earlier rate calibration, which failed its cross-energy check.Published work establishes Si–C bond inversion and controlled Si motion as real starting operations. It also establishes competing configurations and pathways. It does not establish that every visually successful Si displacement transports the expected carbon isotope. In particular, an adatom excursion followed by refilling cannot be classified from the final Si position alone. arxiv.org+1Parking helps separate the reader from the most reactive neighbourhood. It does not by itself establish that the parked Si has negligible mechanical influence, that the entire parking-path inventory survived, or that long spectroscopy exposures are passive.A mass witness derived from the scattering interactionThe useful readout result is an energy-moment identity.Let the nuclear Hamiltonian beH=∑ipi22Mi+V(R),H=\sum_i\frac{\mathbf p_i^2}{2M_i}+V(\mathbf R),H=i∑​2Mi​pi2​​+V(R),and let A(R)A(\mathbf R)A(R) be the position-dependent electron-scattering operator for a specified incident probe and outgoing detector channel. For a stationary, time-reversal-invariant nuclear state, the full signed nuclear energy-transfer spectrum obeys∫−∞∞E SA(E) dE=ℏ22∑i⟨∣∇iA∣2⟩Mi.\boxed{
