ration-free pupil are calculation choices; I am not attributing that complete configuration to the published experiment. In particular, the reported 5.8 meV on-axis resolution cannot be substituted for the 28 meV off-axis resolution while retaining the rest of this calculation. n11.iphy.ac.cnThe model includes nuclear thermal motion, coherent probe amplitudes, detector weighting and the isotope-dependent normal modes. It omits out-of-plane motion, reactive Si interactions, electronic inelastic background, actual probe aberrations and detector inefficiency. Translation modes are excluded in the finite-cell covariance calculation. Those qualifications prevent its numerical output from becoming an experimentally calibrated reader.Predicted mass and material signalsFor the two-label test configuration, the calculated changes are:Probe positionIntended local isotope changeChange in full nuclear energy momentChange in optical-band centroidaaa13C→12C^{13}\mathrm C\rightarrow{}^{12}\mathrm C13C→12Cabout +7.8%about +2.05 meVfff12C→13C^{12}\mathrm C\rightarrow{}^{13}\mathrm C12C→13Cabout −7.2%about −2.09 meVbbbremains 12C^{12}\mathrm C12Cabout +0.08%about +0.677 meVThe last column is the centroid of the calculated one-phonon electron-scattering component, convolved with the stated resolution and integrated over 145–210 meV. It is not a prediction for an unprocessed experimental spectrum containing all backgrounds and multiphonon contributions.The distinction at bbb is useful: its own isotope stays fixed, its mass-sensitive moment changes very little, and its optical response changes because its neighbourhood changes. Before applying electron-scattering weights, the local projected vibrational density of states gave a shift of approximately +0.57+0.57+0.57 to +0.62+0.62+0.62 meV across the two published force-model fits. Applying the coherent scattering weights gives approximately +0.677+0.677+0.677 meV in the main model. Increasing the angular quadrature changed that result b