constructions, not ideal comparators granted free assembly or readout. arxiv.org+3Single-isotope perturbations of spatial vibrational spectra also have explicit first-principles precedent. The possible new operation lies in controlled post-assembly intervention and verification, not in the existence of an isotope-dependent vibrational response. PubMedThe mass witness survives, with substantial physical costsFor a before/after mass-moment measurement at a changed site, the calculation gives a diagnostic requirement of approximately8.9×1098.9\times10^98.9×109primary electrons for each spectrum. At 7 pA this is approximately 3.4 minutes per spectrum. Reading both changed positions before and after therefore takes approximately 13.5 minutes of ideal beam time.The associated peak fluence is approximately1.2×1010 electrons/A˚21.2\times10^{10}\ \mathrm{electrons/\mathring A^2}1.2×1010 electrons/A˚2per spectrum.This estimate uses a variance bound within the specified model and a normal-approximation separation criterion. It is neither a calibrated 99% experimental identification probability nor a fundamental minimum dose.The ordinary 0.6770.6770.677 meV optical-centroid comparison at bbb is more expensive: approximately 4.3×10104.3\times10^{10}4.3×1010 primary electrons, or 16 minutes, for each of the two ideal spectra. Thus even the readily visible model response entails appreciable exposure.Published recoil-readout work reports routine pristine-graphene stability around 108 electrons/A˚210^8\ \mathrm{electrons/\mathring A^2}108 electrons/A˚2 under its conditions, while explicitly allowing that stability could extend further. That is not a destruction threshold. It also does not establish survival through the larger exposures calculated here. arxiv.orgThree interfaces remain particularly important.The energy window must contain the observable being usedIn a harmonic self-scattering benchmark at the central momentum transfer, restricting the nuclear spectrum to ∣E∣<0.3|E|<0.3∣E∣<0.3 eV misses approximately 8% of its first energy moment. That is comparable to the isotope signal itself.Expanding to ∣E∣<0.8|E|<0.8∣E∣<0.8 eV reduces the missing moment to approximately 10−510^{-5}10−5 in that benchmark. This is a central-momentum self-scattering result, not a bound for the whole focused-probe experiment.A wider window introduces a need to establish the electronic background and its fluctuations. A background that cancels in a mean difference can still contribute substantial noise. The published spectra and source workbooks I inspected provide useful phonon shapes and relative intensities, but do not provide the absolute detector statistics needed to close this calculation.Therefore a plotted optical spectrum cannot simply be integrated and declared a mass measurement.Energy referencing is part of the operationAn energy-origin error δE\delta EδE changes the measured first moment byδM1=δE M0,\delta M_1=\delta E\,M_0,δM1​=δEM0​,where M0M_0M0​ is the collected nuclear scattering intensity.Using the deliberately loose intensity bound from the calculation, keeping this bias below one-tenth of the isotope signal would correspond to an energy-origin error near 0.030.030.03 meV. That number is not a universal instrumental requirement: the bound is loose, and shared reference measurements can cancel common offsets.For example,M1,aM0,f−M1,fM0,aM_{1,a}M_{0,f}-M_{1,f}M_{0,a}M1,a​M0,f​−M1,f​M0,a​cancels an exactly common energy offset algebraically. But actual intensity normalization, angle-dependent distortions and time-dependent drift still have to be established. The algebra does not supply their calibration.Parking must be judged by accumulated halo doseThe relevant exposure of the parked reactive neighbourhood isΛpark=∑jσj∫Φj(t) dt,\Lambda_{\rm park}
