\sum_j \sigma_j\int \Phi_j(t)\,dt,Λpark​=j∑​σj​∫Φj​(t)dt,not simply the distance between the probe maximum and Si.The ideal pupil used here admits a small-halo window for the explicit parking path. That is encouraging within the model. It is not transferable to an actual microscope from its nominal probe width alone. A published graphene experiment found substantial broad probe tails and less than 21% of the integrated beam intensity within the probe FWHM; transformations frequently occurred while the beam maximum was away from the defect. Nature CommunicationsA measured intensity profile is also insufficient to reconstruct the complete coherent probe phase used by the read calculation.What this changes about the carbon-transfer questionThe useful prospective operation is now more precise:Use a returned-mediator cycle to attempt a local isotope rearrangement, then test the resulting mass distribution independently of the Si-position record.If acquired, that readout would allow functional mass transfer to be accepted without requiring every microscopic trajectory to preserve the identity of the same carbon atom. An excursion followed by recovery of the correct isotope could be acceptable for a mass-patterning function. A visually identical endpoint produced by wrong-isotope refilling would fail.That distinction matters because the published direct-exchange trajectory and the competing adatom/refilling pathways are not interchangeable evidence. Their existence does not provide the conditional probabilities of correct transfer, own-atom recapture, wrong-isotope replacement or irreversible loss in the intended operation. I have not supplied those probabilities. arxiv.orgThere is an additional scope limit: reading a,b,fa,b,fa,b,f would certify only the tested local output, subject to calibration. It would not certify the complete isotope inventory along every visited site. The explicit closed route plus parking path visits a substantially larger region. A full inventory claim would require additional observations, with extra exposure and special difficulty near the parked Si. Those costs cannot be hidden inside a three-site acceptance test.Likewise, the finite carbon force model does not show that the remote Si contribution cancels exactly. Returning it to the same position removes a direct change of elemental geometry between the two reads, but its presence can still modify the sensitivity to the isotope intervention. Out-of-plane motion, support conditions and the actual Si–C force field must enter a quantitative prediction for the assembled system.Scientific choice at this checkpointI would sustain this opening around certified carbon flow and reversible local isotope intervention, with the mass witness as the enabling uncertainty. I would not invest the next extended unit in a larger permutation construction or in refining this small motif’s optical centroid.The completed work establishes four things:A source-based carbon force model and a finite coherent electron probe produce isotope information without an assigned contrast function.The same model predicts a distinct response at an atom whose own mass stays unchanged.A stronger static-label comparison reproduces most of that response, sharply reducing its present materials significance.Full-spectrum collection, energy referencing, accumulated exposure and unverified reaction branches remain consequential parts of the operation.The attractive capability remains a controlled intervention in one existing covalent neighbourhood, with an independent record of its mass outcome. The present calculation makes that capability testable and exposes its costs. It does not yet establish a usable isotope writer, a thermal-material advantage, or reliable carbon-transfer chemistry.
